    "error_map": {
        "401:1": "auth_expired",
        "401:2": "auth_expired",
        "403:3": "permission_denied",
        "403:29": "permission_denied",
        "400:12": "provider_transient",
        "404:1000": "invalid_request",
        "404:1001": "invalid_request",
        "404:1018": "invalid_request",
        "404:1020": "invalid_request"
    },
    "_error_map_note": "Story 26.3 (dossier section 7): keys are '<http_status>:<body_code>' -- Pinterest v5 error bodies are {\"code\": <int>, \"message\": <str>} and the discrimination is on the BODY code, never the HTTP status alone. THE trap: 400 code 12 ('Retry after ...') is a TRANSIENT 400 -> provider_transient (a naive 400=invalid_request would break the breaker and fire false pull_invalid_request_drift). 401 codes 1/2 = token invalid/expired -> auth_expired (reconnect). 403 code 3 = app consumer type not supported, 403 code 29 = Trial-tier feature not granted -> permission_denied. 429 code 8 is NOT in this map: it stays on the core.quota.RateLimitError breaker path honoring the x-ratelimit-reset header. 5xx fall through to the pure-HTTP provider_transient classification. A 403 'Request has expired' on the async download URL is DownloadLinkExpired (core.async_reports single-resubmission path), not an error_map entry. Any OTHER 400 on a cataloged column is the catalog drift signal (pull_invalid_request_drift).",
    "refetch": {
        "nightly_days": 7,
        "weekly_days": 30,
        "monthly_days": 60,
        "_note": "Dossier section 4.3: Pinterest publishes NO freshness SLA; conversion metrics restate while the attribution windows run (pinned spec 30 click / 30 engagement / 1 view, TIME_OF_AD_ACTION) and delivery metrics stabilize in ~24-72h. nightly 7d catches routine restatement, weekly 30d covers the click/engagement windows, monthly 60d covers the maximum configurable window."
    },
